  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a2ed69b8",
   "metadata": {
    "origin_pos": 0
   },
   "source": [
    "# Forward Propagation, Backward Propagation, and Computational Graphs\n",
    "\n",
    "So far, we have trained our models\n",
    "with minibatch stochastic gradient descent.\n",
    "However, when we implemented the algorithm,\n",
    "we only worried about the calculations involved\n",
    "in *forward propagation* through the model.\n",
    "When it came time to calculate the gradients,\n",
    "we just invoked the backpropagation function provided by the deep learning framework.\n",
    "\n",
    "The automatic calculation of gradients\n",
    "profoundly simplifies\n",
    "the implementation of deep learning algorithms.\n",
    "Before automatic differentiation,\n",
    "even small changes to complicated models required\n",
    "recalculating complicated derivatives by hand.\n",
    "Surprisingly often, academic papers had to allocate\n",
    "numerous pages to deriving update rules.\n",
    "While we must continue to rely on automatic differentiation\n",
    "so we can focus on the interesting parts,\n",
    "you ought to know how these gradients\n",
    "are calculated under the hood\n",
    "if you want to go beyond a shallow\n",
    "understanding of deep learning.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Forward Propagation\n",
    "\n",
    "*Forward propagation* (or *forward pass*) refers to the calculation and storage\n",
    "of network output and intermediate variables.\n",
    "Below we will introduce the concept of a \"computational graph\" to represent the forward propagation of a neural network.  To introduce the concept we will first define a simple network with a single hidden layer.\n",
    "\n",
    "![An MLP with a hidden layer of five hidden units.](https://github.com/d2l-ai/d2l-en/raw/refs/heads/master/img/mlp.svg)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "For simplicity, let's assume that the input example is $\\mathbf{x}\\in \\mathbb{R}^d$\n",
    "and that our hidden layer does not include a bias term.\n",
    "Here the intermediate variable is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{z}= \\mathbf{W}^{(1)} \\mathbf{x},$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\mathbf{W}^{(1)} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{h \\times d}$ is the weight matrix of the hidden layer.\n",
    "After running the intermediate variable $\\mathbf{z}\\in \\mathbb{R}^h$ through the\n",
    "activation function $\\phi$ we obtain our hidden activation vector of length $h$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{h}= \\phi (\\mathbf{z}).$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Assuming that the parameters of the output layer are a weight matrix\n",
    "$\\mathbf{W}^{(2)} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{q \\times h}$,\n",
    "we obtain the output layer as a vector of length $q$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{o}= \\mathbf{W}^{(2)} \\mathbf{h}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Assuming that the loss function is $l$\n",
    "and the example label is $y$,\n",
    "we can then calculate the loss term\n",
    "for a single data example as\n",
    "\n",
    "$$L = l(\\mathbf{o}, y).$$\n",
    "\n",
    "We will add to this an $\\ell_2$ regularization term with hyperparameter $\\lambda$,\n",
    "defined by:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$s = \\frac{\\lambda}{2} \\left(\\|\\mathbf{W}^{(1)}\\|_\\textrm{F}^2 + \\|\\mathbf{W}^{(2)}\\|_\\textrm{F}^2\\right),$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where the Frobenius norm of the matrix\n",
    "is simply the $\\ell_2$ norm applied\n",
    "after flattening the matrix into a vector.\n",
    "Finally, the model's regularized loss\n",
    "on a given data example is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$J = L + s.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "We refer to $J$ as the *objective function* in the following discussion."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "78688ee0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Representing networks with computational graphs\n",
    "\n",
    "Plotting *computational graphs* helps us visualize\n",
    "the dependencies of operators\n",
    "and variables within the calculation.\n",
    "The figure below shows the graph associated\n",
    "with the simple network described above,\n",
    "where squares denote variables and circles denote operators.\n",
    "The lower-left corner signifies the input\n",
    "and the upper-right corner is the output.\n",
    "You'll notice that this graph can be described as a directed acyclic graph (DAG).\n",
    "\n",
    "![Computational graph of forward propagation.](https://github.com/d2l-ai/d2l-en/raw/refs/heads/master/img/forward.svg)\n",
    "\n",
    "Computational graphs are central to how deep learning works. Here's why we care about them:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Representation of a computation**\n",
    "   * A computational graph is a way to represent a complex function (like a neural network) as a sequence of simple operations (addition, multiplication, activation functions, etc.).\n",
    "   * Each node represents an operation or a variable, and edges represent data (tensors) flowing between them.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Automatic differentiation (backpropagation)**\n",
    "   * The graph structure makes it easy to apply the chain rule systematically to compute gradients.\n",
    "   * During the forward pass, values are computed and stored. During the backward pass, the graph is traversed in reverse to compute gradients efficiently.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Modularity and flexibility**\n",
    "   * Graphs let you break down models into reusable building blocks (layers, functions).\n",
    "   * Frameworks like TensorFlow, PyTorch, and JAX rely on computational graphs to define and manage these structures.\n",
    "\n",
    "4. **Performance**\n",
    "   * Graphs allow compilers and runtime environments to optimize computations e.g., by parallelizing operations, or running parts on GPUs/TPUs.\n",
    "\n",
    "5. **Debugging & Visualization**\n",
    "   * Graphs can be visualized to understand model structure.\n",
    "   * This helps researchers verify architectures and spot inefficiencies.\n",
    "\n",
    "In short: computational graphs give us a *blueprint* of the model that enables **automatic differentiation, optimization, and efficient execution**, all the things that make deep learning practical."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6cdf3f00",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Drawing computational graphs\n",
    "\n",
    "Rather than rely on external images, let's build a small helper so we can draw\n",
    "computational graphs directly in the notebook. Squares (variables) hold values;\n",
    "circles (operators) combine them. We'll reuse this to visualize both the forward\n",
    "values and the backward gradients."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "a299fb57",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from matplotlib.patches import FancyArrowPatch, Circle, FancyBboxPatch\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def draw_node(ax, xy, text, shape='circle', color='#e8f0fe', r=0.45):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Draw a single graph node (square = variable, circle = operator).\"\"\"\n",
    "    x, y = xy\n",
    "    if shape == 'circle':\n",
    "        ax.add_patch(Circle((x, y), r, facecolor=color, edgecolor='#333', zorder=3, lw=1.5))\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        # draw the box symmetrically about (x, y) so the label sits at its center\n",
    "        # (boxstyle padding expands the patch equally on all sides, preserving the center)\n",
    "        bw, bh = r, 0.5 * r          # half-width and half-height of the box\n",
    "        ax.add_patch(FancyBboxPatch((x - bw, y - bh), 2 * bw, 2 * bh,\n",
    "                     boxstyle=\"round,pad=0.02\", facecolor=color, edgecolor='#333',\n",
    "                     zorder=3, lw=1.5))\n",
    "    ax.text(x, y, text, ha='center', va='center', fontsize=11, zorder=4)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def draw_edge(ax, a, b, label=None, r=0.45, label_color='#b00'):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Draw a directed edge a -> b, trimmed so the arrow touches the node borders.\"\"\"\n",
    "    a = np.array(a, float); b = np.array(b, float)\n",
    "    u = (b - a) / np.linalg.norm(b - a)\n",
    "    a2, b2 = a + u * r, b - u * r\n",
    "    ax.add_patch(FancyArrowPatch(a2, b2, arrowstyle='-|>', mutation_scale=14,\n",
    "                                 color='#555', zorder=2, lw=1.3))\n",
    "    if label:\n",
    "        m = (a2 + b2) / 2\n",
    "        ax.text(m[0], m[1] + 0.14, label, ha='center', va='bottom',\n",
    "                fontsize=9, color=label_color, zorder=5)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def new_graph_axes(figsize=(8, 3)):\n",
    "    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=figsize)\n",
    "    ax.axis('off'); ax.set_aspect('equal')\n",
    "    return fig, ax"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8f478c55",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## A concrete example: a scalar computational graph\n",
    "\n",
    "Before tackling matrices, let's make the ideas concrete on the smallest interesting model:\n",
    "**one-dimensional linear regression**. For a single data point $(x, y)$ with parameters\n",
    "$w$ (weight) and $b$ (bias), the squared-error loss is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$f(w, b) = \\big(y - (w x + b)\\big)^2.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Here $x$ and $y$ are data (fixed for a given point) and $w, b$ are the parameters we want\n",
    "gradients for. We decompose the loss into elementary operations, exactly the way a\n",
    "framework would:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$p = w x, \\qquad q = p + b, \\qquad r = y - q, \\qquad f = r^2.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The intermediate $q = wx + b$ is the model's **prediction** $\\hat{y}$, and $r = y - q$ is\n",
    "the **residual**. Each operation is a node in the computational graph below."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "3b128686",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "forward:  p = w*x = 6.0,   q = p + b = 5.0 (prediction),   r = y - q = -1.0 (residual),   f = r^2 = 1.0\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1100x400 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# data and parameters\n",
    "x, y = 3.0, 4.0      # a single data point (fixed)\n",
    "w, b = 2.0, -1.0     # the parameters we will differentiate with respect to\n",
    "\n",
    "# forward pass: compute and STORE every intermediate\n",
    "p = w * x        # multiply\n",
    "q = p + b        # add bias  -> this is the prediction y_hat\n",
    "r = y - q        # residual\n",
    "f = r ** 2       # squared error\n",
    "print(f\"forward:  p = w*x = {p},   q = p + b = {q} (prediction),   \"\n",
    "      f\"r = y - q = {r} (residual),   f = r^2 = {f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = new_graph_axes(figsize=(11, 4))\n",
    "pos = {'w': (0, 3.4), 'x': (0, 2.0), 'b': (0, 0.8), 'y': (0, -0.4),\n",
    "       'p': (2.4, 2.7), 'q': (4.6, 1.8), 'r': (6.8, 0.8), 'f': (9.0, 0.8)}\n",
    "styles = {'w': ('box', '#ffd8a8'), 'x': ('box', '#fff3cd'),\n",
    "          'b': ('box', '#ffd8a8'), 'y': ('box', '#fff3cd'),\n",
    "          'p': ('circle', '#e8f0fe'), 'q': ('circle', '#e8f0fe'),\n",
    "          'r': ('circle', '#e8f0fe'), 'f': ('circle', '#d4edda')}\n",
    "op = {'p': '\\u00d7', 'q': '+', 'r': '\\u2212', 'f': '(\\u00b7)\\u00b2'}\n",
    "for n, (sh, col) in styles.items():\n",
    "    draw_node(ax, pos[n], op.get(n, n), sh, col)\n",
    "for n in ['w', 'x', 'b', 'y']:\n",
    "    ax.text(pos[n][0] - 0.62, pos[n][1], n, ha='right', va='center', fontsize=12)\n",
    "\n",
    "draw_edge(ax, pos['w'], pos['p']); draw_edge(ax, pos['x'], pos['p'], label='p = wx')\n",
    "draw_edge(ax, pos['p'], pos['q']); draw_edge(ax, pos['b'], pos['q'], label='q = p + b')\n",
    "draw_edge(ax, pos['q'], pos['r']); draw_edge(ax, pos['y'], pos['r'], label='r = y \\u2212 q')\n",
    "draw_edge(ax, pos['r'], pos['f'], label='f = r\\u00b2')\n",
    "ax.text(pos['q'][0], pos['q'][1] + 0.6, 'prediction \\u0177', ha='center', fontsize=8,\n",
    "        color='#555', style='italic')\n",
    "ax.text(pos['r'][0], pos['r'][1] + 0.6, 'residual', ha='center', fontsize=8,\n",
    "        color='#555', style='italic')\n",
    "ax.set_xlim(-1.3, 9.8); ax.set_ylim(-1.1, 4.1)\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"Forward pass:  w=2, x=3, b=\\u22121, y=4  \\u2192  f = 1\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout();"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "576b9f3c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### The backward pass, step by step\n",
    "\n",
    "The forward pass gave us $p=6$, $q=5$, $r=-1$, $f=1$. Now we want the gradients with\n",
    "respect to the parameters, $\\partial f/\\partial w$ and $\\partial f/\\partial b$. Computing\n",
    "each from scratch would repeat a lot of work, because they share most of the same path\n",
    "back through $q$, $r$, and $f$. Backpropagation avoids that repetition with one simple,\n",
    "reusable rule.\n",
    "\n",
    "**The rule.** Give every node a number $g$, its *gradient* $\\partial f/\\partial(\\text{node})$\n",
    "-- how much the final output $f$ changes per unit change in that node. Process the nodes\n",
    "from output back to input. For each edge from a parent $p$ into a child $c$,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\underbrace{g_{\\text{parent}}}_{\\text{parent's gradient}} \\mathrel{+}= \\underbrace{g_{\\text{child}}}_{\\substack{\\text{child's gradient}\\\\(\\text{the \"upstream\" gradient})}} \\times \\underbrace{\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial\\,\\text{parent}}}_{\\substack{\\text{local derivative}\\\\ \\text{of the edge}}}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "In words: **a node's gradient is the gradient arriving from above (upstream) times the\n",
    "local derivative of the operation on that edge.** The local derivative depends only on the\n",
    "single operation at that edge -- multiply, add, subtract, square -- so it is easy to write\n",
    "down. We use $\\mathrel{+}=$ (accumulate) rather than $=$ because a node can feed *several*\n",
    "children; we will see why that matters in a moment.\n",
    "\n",
    "We seed the process at the output with $g_f = \\partial f/\\partial f = 1$, and go backward."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8a711cae",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Walking it backward, node by node.** We only differentiate with respect to the\n",
    "*parameters* $w$ and $b$, so we stop propagating once we reach them (there is no need to\n",
    "push gradients into the data inputs $x$ and $y$).\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Start at the output:** $g_f = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial f} = 1$.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Edge $f \\leftarrow r$** (operation $f = r^2$, local derivative $\\partial f/\\partial r = 2r = -2$):\n",
    "   $$g_r = g_f \\cdot 2r = 1 \\cdot (-2) = -2.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Edge $r \\leftarrow q$** (operation $r = y - q$, local derivative $\\partial r/\\partial q = -1$):\n",
    "   $$g_q = g_r \\cdot (-1) = -2 \\cdot (-1) = 2.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "4. **Edges out of $q = p + b$** (local derivatives $\\partial q/\\partial p = 1$, $\\partial q/\\partial b = 1$):\n",
    "   $$g_p = g_q \\cdot 1 = 2, \\qquad \\boxed{g_b = g_q \\cdot 1 = 2}.$$\n",
    "   The gradient for the bias is finished: $\\partial f/\\partial b = 2$.\n",
    "\n",
    "5. **Edge $p \\leftarrow w$** (operation $p = w x$, local derivative $\\partial p/\\partial w = x = 3$):\n",
    "   $$\\boxed{g_w = g_p \\cdot x = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "So the parameter gradients are $\\partial f/\\partial w = 6$ and $\\partial f/\\partial b = 2$.\n",
    "Notice that $g_q = 2$ was computed **once** and then reused for *both* the bias $b$ and the\n",
    "$p\\!\\to\\!w$ branch: reusing shared upstream gradients is exactly what makes backprop\n",
    "efficient. The diagram below shows the local derivative on each edge (blue) and the\n",
    "gradient that ends up at each node (red)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "25cf6059",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1100x420 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from matplotlib.patches import FancyArrowPatch\n",
    "\n",
    "def _bedge(ax, a, b, color, label=None, r=0.42, label_color=None, label_off=0.14, fs=8):\n",
    "    a = np.array(a, float); b = np.array(b, float)\n",
    "    u = (b - a) / np.linalg.norm(b - a)\n",
    "    a2, b2 = a + u * r, b - u * r\n",
    "    ax.add_patch(FancyArrowPatch(a2, b2, arrowstyle='-|>', mutation_scale=12,\n",
    "                 color=color, zorder=2, lw=1.3))\n",
    "    if label:\n",
    "        m = (a2 + b2) / 2\n",
    "        ax.text(m[0], m[1] + label_off, label, ha='center', va='bottom',\n",
    "                fontsize=fs, color=label_color or color, zorder=5)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = new_graph_axes(figsize=(11, 4.2))\n",
    "pos = {'w': (0, 3.4), 'x': (0, 2.0), 'b': (0, 0.8), 'y': (0, -0.4),\n",
    "       'p': (2.4, 2.7), 'q': (4.6, 1.8), 'r': (6.8, 0.8), 'f': (9.0, 0.8)}\n",
    "styles = {'w': ('box', '#ffd8a8'), 'x': ('box', '#fff3cd'),\n",
    "          'b': ('box', '#ffd8a8'), 'y': ('box', '#fff3cd'),\n",
    "          'p': ('circle', '#e8f0fe'), 'q': ('circle', '#e8f0fe'),\n",
    "          'r': ('circle', '#e8f0fe'), 'f': ('circle', '#d4edda')}\n",
    "op = {'p': '\\u00d7', 'q': '+', 'r': '\\u2212', 'f': '(\\u00b7)\\u00b2'}\n",
    "for n, (sh, col) in styles.items():\n",
    "    draw_node(ax, pos[n], op.get(n, n), sh, col)\n",
    "for n in ['w', 'x', 'b', 'y']:\n",
    "    ax.text(pos[n][0] - 0.62, pos[n][1], n, ha='right', va='center', fontsize=12)\n",
    "\n",
    "GRAY, RED, BLUE = '#bbb', '#c0392b', '#2c6fbb'\n",
    "\n",
    "# forward edges, each labelled with the LOCAL derivative (blue)\n",
    "# label only the edges whose local derivative we actually use (parameter paths);\n",
    "# the data-input edges (x->p, y->r) are drawn but left unlabelled\n",
    "fwd = [('w', 'p', \"\\u2202p/\\u2202w = x = 3\"), ('x', 'p', None),\n",
    "       ('p', 'q', \"\\u2202q/\\u2202p = 1\"), ('b', 'q', \"\\u2202q/\\u2202b = 1\"),\n",
    "       ('q', 'r', \"\\u2202r/\\u2202q = \\u22121\"), ('y', 'r', None),\n",
    "       ('r', 'f', \"\\u2202f/\\u2202r = 2r = \\u22122\")]\n",
    "for src_node, dst_node, lbl in fwd:\n",
    "    _bedge(ax, pos[src_node], pos[dst_node], GRAY, label=lbl, label_color=BLUE, label_off=0.14)\n",
    "\n",
    "# the gradient that ends up at each node (red), placed just below the node\n",
    "# show the accumulated gradient under each node we differentiate through\n",
    "# (the data inputs x, y get no gradient -- we don't propagate into them)\n",
    "grad = {'f': 'g = 1', 'r': 'g = \\u22122', 'q': 'g = 2', 'p': 'g = 2',\n",
    "        'w': '\\u2202f/\\u2202w = 6', 'b': '\\u2202f/\\u2202b = 2'}\n",
    "for n, gl in grad.items():\n",
    "    ax.text(pos[n][0], pos[n][1] - 0.58, gl, ha='center', va='top',\n",
    "            fontsize=8.5, color=RED, zorder=5)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.plot([], [], color=BLUE, lw=1.5, label='local derivative (on edge)')\n",
    "ax.plot([], [], color=RED, lw=1.5, label='gradient  g = \\u2202f/\\u2202(node)')\n",
    "ax.legend(loc='upper center', bbox_to_anchor=(0.5, 1.14), ncol=2, fontsize=8, frameon=False)\n",
    "ax.set_xlim(-1.3, 9.8); ax.set_ylim(-1.3, 4.3)\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"Backward pass: node gradient = incoming gradient \\u00d7 local derivative\", pad=18)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout();"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a15d4f8c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Connection to the chain rule\n",
    "\n",
    "The node-by-node rule is nothing more than the **chain rule**, organized so that shared\n",
    "sub-expressions are computed once. To see this, expand $\\partial f/\\partial w$ the classical\n",
    "way, multiplying the local derivative of every operation along the path from $f$ back to $w$\n",
    "($f \\leftarrow r \\leftarrow q \\leftarrow p \\leftarrow w$):\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial w}\n",
    "  = \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial r}\\,\\frac{\\partial r}{\\partial q}\\,\\frac{\\partial q}{\\partial p}\\,\\frac{\\partial p}{\\partial w}\n",
    "  = (2r)(-1)(1)(x)\n",
    "  = (-2)(-1)(1)(3) = 6.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Likewise, the path from $f$ back to $b$ is $f \\leftarrow r \\leftarrow q \\leftarrow b$, giving\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial b}\n",
    "  = \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial r}\\,\\frac{\\partial r}{\\partial q}\\,\\frac{\\partial q}{\\partial b}\n",
    "  = (2r)(-1)(1)\n",
    "  = (-2)(-1)(1) = 2.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "These match the values we found by walking the graph. Now compare the two products: they\n",
    "**share the leading factor** $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial r}\\frac{\\partial r}{\\partial q} = (2r)(-1) = 2$.\n",
    "That shared factor is exactly the gradient $g_q = 2$ we computed at node $q$. Backprop's rule\n",
    "$g_{\\text{parent}} = g_{\\text{child}} \\times (\\text{local derivative})$ is just this chain-rule\n",
    "product built up **incrementally from the output**, so each shared prefix (like $g_q$) is\n",
    "evaluated a single time and then reused by every parameter downstream of it. For a network\n",
    "with millions of parameters, that reuse is the difference between feasible and hopeless.\n",
    "\n",
    "**When paths branch.** Here every parameter reaches $f$ by a single path. If a node were\n",
    "reached by **several** paths (as happens for a weight shared across positions, or for a\n",
    "hidden unit feeding many outputs), the chain rule sums over the paths:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta} = \\sum_{\\text{paths } \\theta \\to f}\\ \\prod_{\\text{edges on path}} (\\text{local derivative}),$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and the accumulation ($\\mathrel{+}=$) in our rule is precisely what adds those paths together.\n",
    "This is the \"sum over paths\" form of the multivariate chain rule that we will apply to the\n",
    "full network next.\n",
    "\n",
    "Now let's run the same computation in code, propagating the gradient backward node by node."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "9c1bfd9b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "intermediates: g_f=1.0, g_r=-2.0, g_q=2.0, g_p=2.0\n",
      "parameters:    df/dw = 6.0,   df/db = 2.0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# backward pass by hand: give each node a gradient g = df/d(node),\n",
    "# and apply  g_parent = g_child * (local derivative)  from output back to inputs.\n",
    "# We only care about the parameters w and b, so we stop there (no g_x, g_y).\n",
    "g_f = 1.0                    # seed: df/df\n",
    "g_r = g_f * (2 * r)          # through f = r^2      (local derivative 2r)\n",
    "g_q = g_r * (-1.0)           # through r = y - q    (dr/dq = -1)\n",
    "g_p = g_q * 1.0              # through q = p + b    (dq/dp = 1)\n",
    "g_b = g_q * 1.0              # through q = p + b    (dq/db = 1)\n",
    "g_w = g_p * x                # through p = w * x    (dp/dw = x)\n",
    "\n",
    "# the node-gradients on the parameters are the ones we want\n",
    "df_dw, df_db = g_w, g_b\n",
    "print(f\"intermediates: g_f={g_f}, g_r={g_r}, g_q={g_q}, g_p={g_p}\")\n",
    "print(f\"parameters:    df/dw = {df_dw},   df/db = {df_db}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "cc70aacd",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "autograd: df/dw = 6.0,   df/db = 2.0\n",
      "[OK] manual backprop matches autograd\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import torch\n",
    "\n",
    "# verify against autograd. We only need gradients for the parameters, so only\n",
    "# w and b require grad; x and y are treated as constant data.\n",
    "wt = torch.tensor(w, requires_grad=True)\n",
    "bt = torch.tensor(b, requires_grad=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "f_t = (y - (wt * x + bt)) ** 2\n",
    "f_t.backward()   # traverses the graph in reverse, filling wt.grad and bt.grad\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"autograd: df/dw = {wt.grad.item()},   df/db = {bt.grad.item()}\")\n",
    "assert abs(df_dw - wt.grad.item()) < 1e-6\n",
    "assert abs(df_db - bt.grad.item()) < 1e-6\n",
    "print(\"[OK] manual backprop matches autograd\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "eb2f9d71",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Backpropagation\n",
    "\n",
    "How do we calculate the gradient of a multi-layer neural network?\n",
    "In the case of our simple network, this is the function\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{W}^{(2)} \\phi(\\mathbf{W}^{(1)} \\mathbf{x}).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This entails calculating derivatives with respect to $\\mathbf{W}^{(1)}$ and\n",
    "$\\mathbf{W}^{(2)}$. The derivative with respect to $\\mathbf{W}^{(2)}$ is\n",
    "straightforward, but the derivative with respect to $\\mathbf{W}^{(1)}$ requires the\n",
    "*chain rule*.\n",
    "\n",
    "### The multivariate chain rule\n",
    "\n",
    "For functions of a single variable, if $z = f(y)$ and $y = g(x)$, then\n",
    "$\\frac{dz}{dx} = \\frac{dz}{dy}\\frac{dy}{dx}$. When a variable influences the output\n",
    "through **several** intermediate paths, we must *sum* the contribution of every path.\n",
    "If $z$ depends on $y_1, \\dots, y_n$, each of which depends on $x$, then\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y_i}\\,\\frac{\\partial y_i}{\\partial x}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This \"sum over paths\" is the single most important fact behind backpropagation: a\n",
    "parameter deep in the network reaches the loss through many routes, and its gradient\n",
    "accumulates a term from each one.\n",
    "\n",
    "For networks with multiple layers, this calculation is performed by traversing the\n",
    "graph in reverse order, from the output back to the input. That algorithm is\n",
    "**backpropagation**.\n",
    "\n",
    "The algorithm was proposed independently several times, notably:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Rumelhart, D. E.; Hinton, G. E.; Williams, R. J. (1986). \"Learning representations by back-propagating errors.\" *Nature* 323 (6088): 533\\u2013536.\n",
    "* Werbos, P. J. (1974). *Beyond Regression: New Tools for Prediction and Analysis in the Behavioral Sciences.* PhD thesis, Harvard University."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "390d5a8e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Backprop for the MLP, in matrix form\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's derive the gradients for the network from the *Forward Propagation* section. To keep\n",
    "the algebra readable we use a single training example and squared-error loss, and we write\n",
    "the objective as $J = L + s$ with\n",
    "\n",
    "$$L = \\tfrac12\\,\\lVert \\mathbf{o} - \\mathbf{y}\\rVert^2, \\qquad\n",
    "  s = \\tfrac{\\lambda}{2}\\left(\\lVert\\mathbf{W}^{(1)}\\rVert_F^2 + \\lVert\\mathbf{W}^{(2)}\\rVert_F^2\\right).$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Recall the forward pass:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{z} = \\mathbf{W}^{(1)}\\mathbf{x}, \\qquad\n",
    "  \\mathbf{h} = \\phi(\\mathbf{z}), \\qquad\n",
    "  \\mathbf{o} = \\mathbf{W}^{(2)}\\mathbf{h}, \\qquad\n",
    "  J = L(\\mathbf{o},\\mathbf{y}) + s.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Now walk backward. Write $\\boldsymbol{\\delta}$ for the gradient of $J$ with respect to\n",
    "each intermediate (\"the upstream gradient\"):\n",
    "\n",
    "**Output.** Since $L = \\tfrac12\\lVert\\mathbf{o}-\\mathbf{y}\\rVert^2$,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\frac{\\partial J}{\\partial \\mathbf{o}} = \\mathbf{o} - \\mathbf{y} \\;=:\\; \\boldsymbol{\\delta}_o.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Second weight matrix.** Because $\\mathbf{o} = \\mathbf{W}^{(2)}\\mathbf{h}$, and adding the\n",
    "regularizer's $\\lambda \\mathbf{W}^{(2)}$ term,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\frac{\\partial J}{\\partial \\mathbf{W}^{(2)}}\n",
    "   = \\boldsymbol{\\delta}_o\\,\\mathbf{h}^\\top + \\lambda\\,\\mathbf{W}^{(2)}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Into the hidden layer.** Propagating through $\\mathbf{o}=\\mathbf{W}^{(2)}\\mathbf{h}$,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\frac{\\partial J}{\\partial \\mathbf{h}} = \\big(\\mathbf{W}^{(2)}\\big)^\\top \\boldsymbol{\\delta}_o.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Through the activation.** With $\\mathbf{h} = \\phi(\\mathbf{z})$, the gradient is scaled\n",
    "elementwise by $\\phi'(\\mathbf{z})$ (for ReLU, $\\phi'(z)=\\mathbf{1}[z>0]$):\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\boldsymbol{\\delta}_z \\;=\\; \\frac{\\partial J}{\\partial \\mathbf{z}}\n",
    "   = \\frac{\\partial J}{\\partial \\mathbf{h}} \\odot \\phi'(\\mathbf{z}).$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**First weight matrix.** Finally, since $\\mathbf{z} = \\mathbf{W}^{(1)}\\mathbf{x}$,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\frac{\\partial J}{\\partial \\mathbf{W}^{(1)}}\n",
    "   = \\boldsymbol{\\delta}_z\\,\\mathbf{x}^\\top + \\lambda\\,\\mathbf{W}^{(1)}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Notice the pattern: each weight-matrix gradient is an **outer product** of the upstream\n",
    "gradient at that layer's output with the input that fed into it, and the upstream gradient\n",
    "is passed further back by multiplying with $\\big(\\mathbf{W}\\big)^\\top$. That regular\n",
    "structure is exactly what makes backprop easy to implement as a loop over layers."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c3004634",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Implementing backprop from scratch\n",
    "\n",
    "Now let's put the derivation to work: we'll implement the forward and backward passes of\n",
    "the two-layer MLP **by hand**, then confirm the hand-computed gradients agree with\n",
    "PyTorch's autograd to machine precision. This is the payoff \\u2014 once you can reproduce\n",
    "autograd, you understand what it does \"under the hood.\\\""
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "id": "79264570",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "shapes:  x (4,)  W1 (5, 4)  W2 (3, 5)  y (3,)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import torch\n",
    "\n",
    "torch.manual_seed(0)\n",
    "\n",
    "# dimensions and a single training example\n",
    "d, h_dim, q = 4, 5, 3       # input / hidden / output sizes\n",
    "lam = 0.1                    # L2 regularization strength\n",
    "\n",
    "x = torch.randn(d)\n",
    "y = torch.randn(q)\n",
    "\n",
    "# parameters (require grad so we can cross-check with autograd)\n",
    "W1 = torch.randn(h_dim, d, requires_grad=True)\n",
    "W2 = torch.randn(q, h_dim, requires_grad=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"shapes:  x\", tuple(x.shape), \" W1\", tuple(W1.shape),\n",
    "      \" W2\", tuple(W2.shape), \" y\", tuple(y.shape))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "26865e15",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Forward pass\n",
    "\n",
    "We compute and **store** each intermediate (`z`, `h`, `o`), because the backward pass\n",
    "needs them."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "id": "5adcb50a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "loss L = 35.0869,  reg s = 1.6823,  objective J = 36.7692\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# forward pass, storing intermediates\n",
    "z = W1 @ x                      # hidden pre-activation\n",
    "h = torch.relu(z)               # hidden activation\n",
    "o = W2 @ h                      # output\n",
    "L = 0.5 * torch.sum((o - y)**2)                       # squared-error loss\n",
    "s = (lam / 2) * (torch.sum(W1**2) + torch.sum(W2**2)) # L2 regularizer\n",
    "J = L + s                                             # objective\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"loss L = {L.item():.4f},  reg s = {s.item():.4f},  objective J = {J.item():.4f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ef253406",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Backward pass, by hand\n",
    "\n",
    "We now apply the matrix formulas derived above, top to bottom, propagating the upstream\n",
    "gradient from the objective back to $\\mathbf{W}^{(1)}$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "id": "81ffc936",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "hand-computed grad_W1 shape: (5, 4)\n",
      "hand-computed grad_W2 shape: (3, 5)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# backward pass implemented from scratch (no autograd)\n",
    "with torch.no_grad():\n",
    "    delta_o = (o - y)                        # dJ/do\n",
    "    grad_W2 = torch.outer(delta_o, h) + lam * W2   # dJ/dW2  (outer product + reg)\n",
    "\n",
    "    dh      = W2.t() @ delta_o               # dJ/dh\n",
    "    delta_z = dh * (z > 0).float()           # dJ/dz  (ReLU derivative gates the gradient)\n",
    "    grad_W1 = torch.outer(delta_z, x) + lam * W1   # dJ/dW1  (outer product + reg)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"hand-computed grad_W1 shape:\", tuple(grad_W1.shape))\n",
    "print(\"hand-computed grad_W2 shape:\", tuple(grad_W2.shape))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "73c1344f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Check against autograd\n",
    "\n",
    "If our derivation is right, `J.backward()` should produce the *same* gradients."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "id": "52902f87",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "max |grad_W1 - autograd| = 0.00e+00\n",
      "max |grad_W2 - autograd| = 0.00e+00\n",
      "[OK] hand-written backprop matches autograd\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "J.backward()   # autograd traverses the graph in reverse and fills W1.grad, W2.grad\n",
    "\n",
    "err_W1 = (grad_W1 - W1.grad).abs().max().item()\n",
    "err_W2 = (grad_W2 - W2.grad).abs().max().item()\n",
    "print(f\"max |grad_W1 - autograd| = {err_W1:.2e}\")\n",
    "print(f\"max |grad_W2 - autograd| = {err_W2:.2e}\")\n",
    "assert err_W1 < 1e-5 and err_W2 < 1e-5\n",
    "print(\"[OK] hand-written backprop matches autograd\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ba801648",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Visualizing the backward flow\n",
    "\n",
    "The diagram below shows the same network as a computational graph. Grey arrows are the\n",
    "forward pass (left to right): the inputs and weights combine into `z`, `h`, `o`, the loss\n",
    "`L`, the regularizer `s`, and finally the objective `J = L + s`. Red dashed arrows are the\n",
    "backward pass (right to left): starting from `J`, the gradient signal flows back along the\n",
    "chain, and at each weight matrix it produces the gradient we derived (shown in red). The\n",
    "upstream gradients are labelled: `delta_o = o - y` at the output, and\n",
    "`delta_z = delta_h * phi\\'(z)` after passing back through the activation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "id": "5d482236",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1200x440 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from matplotlib.patches import FancyArrowPatch\n",
    "\n",
    "def _edge(ax, a, b, color, label=None, curve=0.0, dashed=False, r=0.45,\n",
    "          label_color=None, label_off=0.16, fontsize=8.5):\n",
    "    a = np.array(a, float); b = np.array(b, float)\n",
    "    u = (b - a) / np.linalg.norm(b - a)\n",
    "    a2, b2 = a + u * r, b - u * r\n",
    "    ax.add_patch(FancyArrowPatch(a2, b2, arrowstyle='-|>', mutation_scale=13,\n",
    "                 color=color, zorder=2, lw=1.3,\n",
    "                 connectionstyle=f'arc3,rad={curve}',\n",
    "                 linestyle='--' if dashed else '-'))\n",
    "    if label:\n",
    "        m = (a2 + b2) / 2\n",
    "        perp = np.array([-u[1], u[0]])\n",
    "        p = m + perp * (curve * np.linalg.norm(b - a) * 0.5) + np.array([0, label_off])\n",
    "        ax.text(p[0], p[1], label, ha='center', va='bottom',\n",
    "                fontsize=fontsize, color=label_color or color, zorder=5)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = new_graph_axes(figsize=(12, 4.4))\n",
    "\n",
    "pos = {\n",
    "    'x':  (0.0, 1.4), 'W1': (0.0, 2.8),\n",
    "    'z':  (2.0, 1.4), 'h':  (4.0, 1.4),\n",
    "    'W2': (4.0, 2.8),\n",
    "    'o':  (6.0, 1.4), 'y':  (6.0, 0.0),\n",
    "    'L':  (8.0, 0.9), 's':  (8.0, 2.6),\n",
    "    'J':  (10.0, 1.7),\n",
    "}\n",
    "styles = {\n",
    "    'x': ('box', '#fff3cd'), 'W1': ('box', '#ffd8a8'),\n",
    "    'W2': ('box', '#ffd8a8'), 'y': ('box', '#fff3cd'),\n",
    "    'z': ('circle', '#e8f0fe'), 'h': ('circle', '#e8f0fe'),\n",
    "    'o': ('circle', '#e8f0fe'), 'L': ('circle', '#e8f0fe'),\n",
    "    's': ('circle', '#e8f0fe'), 'J': ('circle', '#d4edda'),\n",
    "}\n",
    "for n, (sh, col) in styles.items():\n",
    "    draw_node(ax, pos[n], n, sh, col)\n",
    "\n",
    "GRAY, RED = '#888', '#c0392b'\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- forward pass (gray) ----\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['W1'], pos['z'], GRAY)\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['x'],  pos['z'], GRAY, label='z = W\\u2081x')\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['z'],  pos['h'], GRAY, label='h = \\u03c6(z)')\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['W2'], pos['o'], GRAY)\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['h'],  pos['o'], GRAY, label='o = W\\u2082h')\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['o'],  pos['L'], GRAY, label='L = \\u00bd\\u2016o\\u2212y\\u2016\\u00b2')\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['y'],  pos['L'], GRAY)\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['W1'], pos['s'], GRAY, curve=-0.4)          # curved to clear W2\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['W2'], pos['s'], GRAY, label='s = \\u00bd\\u03bb(\\u2016W\\u2081\\u2016\\u00b2+\\u2016W\\u2082\\u2016\\u00b2)')\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['L'],  pos['J'], GRAY, label='J = L + s')\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['s'],  pos['J'], GRAY)\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- backward pass (red, dashed, curved back along the main chain) ----\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['J'], pos['L'], RED, curve=0.35, dashed=True)\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['L'], pos['o'], RED, curve=0.35, dashed=True,\n",
    "      label=r'$\\delta_o = o - y$', label_color=RED, label_off=-0.45)\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['o'], pos['h'], RED, curve=0.35, dashed=True)\n",
    "_edge(ax, pos['h'], pos['z'], RED, curve=0.35, dashed=True,\n",
    "      label=r\"$\\delta_z = \\delta_h \\odot \\phi'(z)$\", label_color=RED, label_off=-0.5)\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- gradient results at the weight matrices (red), placed clear of all nodes ----\n",
    "ax.text(pos['W2'][0] + 1.2, pos['W2'][1] + 0.55,\n",
    "        r\"$\\partial J/\\partial W_2 = \\delta_o h^\\top + \\lambda W_2$\",\n",
    "        color=RED, fontsize=9, ha='center')\n",
    "ax.text(pos['W1'][0] - 0.55, pos['W1'][1] + 0.75,\n",
    "        r\"$\\partial J/\\partial W_1 = \\delta_z x^\\top + \\lambda W_1$\",\n",
    "        color=RED, fontsize=9, ha='left')\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- legend ----\n",
    "ax.plot([], [], color=GRAY, lw=1.5, label='forward pass')\n",
    "ax.plot([], [], color=RED, lw=1.5, ls='--', label='backward pass')\n",
    "ax.legend(loc='lower right', fontsize=9, frameon=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_xlim(-1.3, 12.2); ax.set_ylim(-0.8, 4.1)\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"Forward (gray) and backward (red) through the MLP\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout();"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3f3928b6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Why training needs more memory than prediction\n",
    "\n",
    "Notice that the backward pass reused `z`, `h`, and `o` \\u2014 the intermediates from the\n",
    "forward pass. This is the key coupling between the two passes: **the values computed going\n",
    "forward must be kept in memory until the backward pass consumes them.**\n",
    "\n",
    "At prediction time we only run the forward pass and can discard each intermediate as soon\n",
    "as the next layer is computed. During training we cannot: the gradient formulas need\n",
    "$\\mathbf{h}$ (for $\\partial J/\\partial \\mathbf{W}^{(2)}$), $\\mathbf{z}$ (for the activation\n",
    "derivative), and $\\mathbf{x}$ (for $\\partial J/\\partial \\mathbf{W}^{(1)}$). This is why\n",
    "training a network of a given size requires substantially more memory than running it for\n",
    "inference, and why very deep networks sometimes trade compute for memory via techniques\n",
    "like *gradient checkpointing* (recomputing intermediates in the backward pass instead of\n",
    "storing them)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d0774588",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Local minima\n",
    "\n",
    "What can we say about the error surface of an MLP?  It can have many local minima.  Therefore gradient-based training doesn't necessarily find the global minimum.\n",
    "\n",
    "Is this a problem?  Not necessarily.  Compare this to linear models like linear regression or logistic regression which have a globally optimal solutions.\n",
    "\n",
    "There are other issues that we will discuss in the next notebook!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "91a51b75",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Forward propagation** computes and stores the network's output and every intermediate\n",
    "  variable, moving from input to output through the computational graph.\n",
    "* A **computational graph** expresses the model as elementary operations; its structure is\n",
    "  what lets us apply the chain rule mechanically.\n",
    "* **Backpropagation** computes gradients by traversing that graph in reverse. At each layer\n",
    "  the weight gradient is an outer product of the upstream gradient with the layer's input,\n",
    "  and the upstream gradient is passed further back by multiplying with $\\mathbf{W}^\\top$ and\n",
    "  gating through the activation's derivative.\n",
    "* The **multivariate chain rule** \\u2014 summing gradient contributions over all paths \\u2014 is the\n",
    "  mathematical heart of the algorithm.\n",
    "* Forward and backward passes are **interdependent**: the backward pass consumes the stored\n",
    "  forward intermediates, so training needs more memory than prediction.\n",
    "* We verified all of this by implementing backprop from scratch and matching PyTorch's\n",
    "  autograd to machine precision."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b09655af",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Exercises\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Add biases.** Extend the forward pass to $\\mathbf{z} = \\mathbf{W}^{(1)}\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{b}^{(1)}$\n",
    "   and $\\mathbf{o} = \\mathbf{W}^{(2)}\\mathbf{h} + \\mathbf{b}^{(2)}$. Derive\n",
    "   $\\partial J/\\partial \\mathbf{b}^{(1)}$ and $\\partial J/\\partial \\mathbf{b}^{(2)}$, add\n",
    "   them to the from-scratch backward pass, and check against autograd.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Swap the activation.** Replace ReLU with $\\tanh$. What is $\\phi'(z)$ now, and which\n",
    "   single line of the backward pass changes? Verify numerically.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Batch it.** Generalize the from-scratch implementation from a single example\n",
    "   $\\mathbf{x}$ to a minibatch $\\mathbf{X}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times d}$. Which outer products\n",
    "   become matrix multiplications, and where does the sum over the batch appear?\n",
    "\n",
    "4. **Numerical gradient check.** Without using autograd, estimate $\\partial J/\\partial W^{(1)}_{ij}$\n",
    "   by finite differences, $\\big(J(W_{ij}+\\epsilon) - J(W_{ij}-\\epsilon)\\big)/(2\\epsilon)$,\n",
    "   and compare to your analytic gradient. What value of $\\epsilon$ gives the best agreement,\n",
    "   and why does making $\\epsilon$ too small *hurt*?"
   ]
  }
 ],
